### How I would state the gap

> Prior work has generated gait at user-specified speeds, learned gait latents supervised by speed, and controlled motion timing through structured latent representations. We test a narrower property: whether a **single post-hoc linear direction** in a sequence-level human-motion latent can produce **monotonic changes in calibrated forward walking speed** after decoding, while retaining plausible gait and subject/style characteristics. We evaluate this by subject-wise splits and compare against speed-conditioned generation, timing controls, and a root-velocity ablation.

That wording avoids claiming speed control is unexplored. Your clearest novelty is the combination of **post-hoc linear intervention, physical speed measurement, subject-wise generalization, and selectivity after root-velocity ablation**.

### Two design details to keep explicit

- **Separate cadence from forward progression.** MotionPyramid’s cycle-frequency control is closely related to tempo, but cadence changes do not by themselves prove that decoded pelvis/root speed changed. Report cadence, stride length, and root-path speed separately.
- **Check whether the data retain absolute speed.** A root-centered or stride-normalized representation can remove forward displacement or timing information. GaitEncoder is a useful warning: a latent may predict a speed label while the input representation has normalized away some of the temporal evidence needed for a speed intervention. [medrxiv.org](https://www.medrxiv.org/content/10.64898/2026.07.07.26357479v1.full?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

Your six references are broadly relevant. The most important additions for the literature review are **Sharma & Rombokas (2023)** and **GaitEncoder (2026)**, because both make the prior-work boundary around speed-aware gait latents more precise.
